(* Kaguya, stage 3. Quoted items are terminals: tokens the lexer emits, written
   with the same text. ident, number and string are terminals too, spelled by
   the lexical rules at the end. NL is the endline token. *)

program    ::= { top } exit_stmt

top        ::= statement | func | struct

statement  ::= decl | array | object | assign | field_set | index_set | call | print | if | while

decl       ::= type [ "🔓" ] ident "⟨" expr "⟩"

array      ::= type size [ "🔓" ] ident init

size       ::= "[" number "]"

type       ::= "3⃣" | "6⃣" | "❓"

object     ::= ident [ "🔓" ] ident init

init       ::= "⟨" init_value { "," init_value } "⟩"

init_value ::= init | expr

assign     ::= ident ":=" expr

field_set  ::= ident "." ident { "." ident } ":=" expr

print      ::= "📢" print_value { "," print_value }

print_value ::= string | expr

if         ::= "🤔" expr NL block [ "🙃" NL block ]

while      ::= "🔁" expr NL block

block      ::= "{" NL { statement } [ exit_stmt NL ] "}" NL

exit_stmt  ::= "🚪" expr

func       ::= "🧩" ident params "→" type NL body

member     ::= "🧩" [ "🔓" ] ident params "→" type NL body

params     ::= "(" [ param { "," param } ] ")"

param      ::= type ident | type size [ "🔓" ] ident

body       ::= "{" NL { statement } exit_stmt NL "}" NL

struct     ::= "🧱" ident NL "{" NL field { field } { member } "}" NL

field      ::= ( type [ size ] | ident ) [ "🔓" ] ident NL

expr       ::= arith [ ( "==" | "≠" | "<" | ">" ) arith ]

arith      ::= term { ( "+" | "-" ) term }

term       ::= factor { "*" factor }

factor     ::= number
             | "✅"
             | "❌"
             | "📥"
             | chain
             | "(" expr ")"
             | "!" factor
             | "-" factor

chain      ::= ident { "." ident } [ args | index ]

index      ::= "[" expr "]"

index_set  ::= ident { "." ident } index ":=" expr

call       ::= ident { "." ident } args

args       ::= "(" [ expr { "," expr } ] ")"


(* Lexical rules. Spaces and tabs separate tokens and are otherwise dropped;
   a newline is NL. A glyph is one UTF-8 character with no variation selector.

   ident      ::= letter { letter | digit }        letter is A-Z, a-z or '_'
   number     ::= digit { digit }                  a letter right after is an error
   string     ::= '"' { any byte but '"' and newline } '"'
   keycap     ::= ( "3" | "6" ) U+20E3             3⃣ and 6⃣, resolved in NUMBER
   glyphs     ::= 🔓 🤔 🙃 🔁 🚪 ✅ ❌ ⟨ ⟩ ≠ 🧩 🧱 📥 📢 →
   singles    ::= { } ( ) [ ] + - * ! , . < >
   pairs      ::= ":=" | "=="

   A string has no escapes. One left open at the end of its line is an error
   at its opening quote.


   Line structure is lexical: the lexer gives one token vector per line, the
   parser drops the endline tokens, and a line with no tokens is skipped. So
   one statement per line and blank lines cost no rule here.

   program is what rejects a statement after exit and a program without one:
   exit_stmt sits outside the repetition, so it is required and it is last.
   A block says the same thing one level down. body is a block whose exit_stmt
   is not optional, so every path through a function leaves with a value.
   '{', '}' and '🙃' each stand alone on their line.

   func and struct are only in top, so a '🧩' or '🧱' inside a block is a syntax
   error. member is only in struct. It is func plus an optional '🔓', which
   marks it mutating. Fields come before the members.

   '⟨' '⟩' delimit an initialiser and '{' '}' delimit a block, and neither does
   the other's job. An object's init nests: a structure-typed field takes an
   init of its own, and so does an array field.

   An array is a scalar type followed by size, in a declaration, a field or a
   parameter, never in a result. Its init is the same rule as an object's; one
   nested level is grammatical, and the checker rejects an element that is an
   init. A '🔓' on a parameter is grammatical only after a size: scalar
   parameters stay constant.

   index sits only at the end of a chain, so a[i][j], a[i].x and f()[i] are
   syntax errors: an element is a scalar with nothing to select. ':=' after a
   chain ending in index is index_set.

   A line starting with an ident needs a second token to dispatch. An ident or
   '🔓' after it makes an object. Otherwise it is a chain: ':=' after a plain
   ident is assign, ':=' after a dotted chain is field_set, and a chain ending
   in args is a call. Every other statement still dispatches on one token.

   A chain ends in at most one call, and nothing follows it: a call's result
   is a scalar, so it has no fields to select. args takes full exprs, so a
   call is an argument of another call.

   expr allows at most one comparison, and it binds weaker than arithmetic: the
   second arith is the only place "==", "≠", "<" or ">" can appear, so a second
   comparison is left over after the expression and rejected there. "<" and
   ">" are there for programs, not as the stage 3 feature.

   '!' and unary '-' are in factor and recurse on factor, so they bind tighter
   than every binary operator: !a == b compares !a with b, -a * b is (-a) * b,
   and !!a is a factor twice over. '(' expr ')' is the only way to override the
   precedence that arith and term fix.

   A string is only in print_value: a string anywhere else is a syntax error.

   Types are not in the grammar: 3⃣ a ⟨ ✅ ⟩ parses. They are checked in the
   semantic pass, along with declaration before use, declaration once per
   scope, 🔓 before :=, the argument count and types, the type a function
   leaves with, field existence, chain mutability and the mutating mark, the
   number of values in an array's init, the index type and a literal index
   out of range.

   Scopes are not in the grammar either. A block is one rule here and one frame
   in the semantic pass and one builder position in codegen, and those three do
   not line up: merge belongs to no brace, and an exit ends a basic block
   without popping a frame. *)
